This package implements the "fix qmhub" command.

This fix enables LAMMPS to be used as the MM driver in QM/MM simulations 
through QMHub, a QM/MM interface (https://github.com/panxl/qmhub). QMHub 
implements QM/MM with augmentary charges (QM/MM-AC) to model electrostatics. 
Essentially, a cutoff around the QM region partitions the MM atoms into 
inner and outer MM regions, and a charge projection scheme is used to project
the outer MM charges onto the inner MM atoms, "augmenting" their charges. 
This method may be applied to both periodic and non-periodic systems. For 
more details, see https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0038120.

QMHub allows coupling with a number of QM programs, including Q-Chem and 
ORCA, among others.

The "fix qmhub" command was written to be compatible with the 29Aug2024 and
the 12Jun2025 versions of LAMMPS.

-------------------------------------------------

Installation
============
Aside from installing the source code via "make-yes qmhub" and building the
LAMMPS executable, nothing special must be done in LAMMPS. This package 
requires the QMHub python package (https://github.com/panxl/qmhub), which 
itself requires the helPME library (https://github.com/andysim/helpme) for 
Particle Mesh Ewald calculations. QMHub requires Python version >= 3.8.

Step 1)

Build LAMMPS with the QMHub package via "make-yes qmhub" and compile the
executable with any other settings.

Step 2)

Build the helPME library.

Check requirements and install helPME (https://github.com/andysim/helpme). 
helPME is built with CMake. In the top-level folder, make a build folder 
and compile:

  mkdir -p build
  cd build
  cmake ..
  make helpmelib

Depending on your system, you may need additional CMake flags after "cmake ..",
such as for specifying the locations of required packages and libraries (MKL/
FFTW, Python executables, etc. These may be specified with -DFFTW_INCLUDES, etc.
See the helPME documentation for more details).

Upon successful compilation, this will produce a shared object file located in
build/python/, with a name such as the following:

  helpmelib.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so

This file is required for QMHub.

Step 3)

Install QMHub.

Get the QMHub source code (https://github.com/panxl/qmhub). Note that QMHub 
requires NumPy (https://numpy.org). In the top-level folder, install with

  python -m pip install .

This should install QMHub into your Python's site-packages. Copy the helPME
shared object file to your /path/to/site-packages/qmhub folder. This completes 
the installation of QMHub, which should be ready to use.

Step 4)

Install the QM package. The compatible QM package of choice should be 
installed per its respective documentation.

-------------------------------------------------

Running QM/MM with QMHub and LAMMPS
===================================

QMHub interfaces MM packages with QM packages. For a QM/MM run with QMHub, the
three requirements are a suitable MM package (in this case, LAMMPS), a suitable
QM package, the MM package input files (LAMMPS input file), and a configuration 
file for QMHub named "qmhub.ini". The "qmhub.ini" file has information regarding
the QM/MM model options, the QM package, and the QM package input options. An 
example "qmhub.ini" file is given below (see https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0038120 
for details on the model parameters):

*** Start example qmhub.ini file ***
  [model]
  switching_function = lrec
  cutoff = 10.
  pbc = TRUE
  
  [engine]
  qchem

  [qchem]
  method = pbe0
  basis = 6-31+g*
  scf_convergence = 6
  maxscf = 200
  dft_d = d3_bj
*** End example qmhub.ini file ***

In the LAMMPS input file, the "fix qmhub" command is used as follows:

  group QM ...
  group MM ...
  fix <ID> all qmhub <qm_r_chrg> <qm_r_spin> <atomic_numbers>

Note that "fix qmhub" applies to the group "all." The additional arguments are
defined as follows:

  qm_r_chrg      : total charge of the QM region, int.
  qm_r_spin      : spin multiplicity of the QM region, int.
                   (1 for singlet, 2 for doublet, ...)
  atomic_numbers : list of atomic numbers of atoms in the simulation, ordered
                   by atom type in the simulation.

As an example, if the QM region is a group of water molecules with two atom 
types (1st:O, 2nd:H):

  fix 1 all qmhub 0 1 8 1

where 8 is the atomic number for O, 1 is the atomic number for H. Note if two
types are defined for the same atom, the atomic number must be repeated (ex: 
for water where each H is its own type, with 1st:O, 2nd:H1, 3rd:H2, the list 
of atomic numbers would be 8 1 1).

In the LAMMPS input file, the "fix qmhub" command has several requirements. 
Two groups must be defined, one for the QM region and one for the MM region. 
THESE GROUPS MUST BE NAMED "QM" AND "MM" AND MUST BE DISJOINT. The simulation 
must either be periodic in all three directions or not periodic in any 
direction; take care to ensure the periodicity of the simulation matches the
"pbc=<TRUE/FALSE>" option in the "[model]" section of the "qmhub.ini" file. 
The atom style used in the simulation must support atomic charges as these 
are necessary for QMHub (ex: "atom_style charge") Currently, units must be set 
to "real" for the sake of unit conversions for the SCF gradients. 

The "fix qmhub" command can also print the QM SCF energy in the thermo output 
via "thermo_style custom", ex: 

  variable ESCF equal f_<ID>
  thermo_style custom step temp etotal v_ESCF

where <ID> is the fix ID for the "fix qmhub" command. As long as the QM package 
may be run from the directory where LAMMPS will be run, the QM/MM job may be 
executed like a normal LAMMPS run, ex:

  mpirun -np <N> /path/to/lmp_executable -in in.lammps > out.lammps

Note that parallel MM is supported.

Upon running, along with the usual LAMMPS output/log files, a directory named
"qmhub" will be created in the same directory where LAMMPS is run. QMHub uses 
this directory to communicate with the QM package, currently through text. 
LAMMPS creates a "qmmm.inp" file containing the QM/MM options, as well as the 
charges and positions of the QM and MM atoms and the cell vectors. This is read 
by QMHub, which will compute the augmentary charges, write the input file for 
the QM package, and run the QM package. Once the QM package completes its 
calculations, QMHub will read the QM output and write a "qmmm.out" file, which 
contains the SCF energy (Ha) and the SCF gradients at each atomic position 
(Ha/Bohr). These are read by LAMMPS, the gradients are converted to forces in 
the proper units, and these QM forces are added to the atoms in the simulation. 
This occurs at each timestep. Any relevant QM package input/output files will 
be located in the "qmhub" directory.
